course: dictionary
lesson: 8143
title: Reproach: disappointment and blame
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A reproach is a way of showing someone that you are disappointed in them. You can give a reproach with words or with a look. «The mother looked at her son with reproach» means she was not pleased with what he did, and she showed it on her face without shouting.
explain: Reproach can also be a verb: to reproach someone is to tell them, gently or sadly, that you are disappointed in what they did. Look at this sentence: «The Rat paid no heed to his doleful self-reproaches.» A self-reproach is blaming yourself. Doleful means sad, so the Rat was sad about blaming himself.
explain: Context helps again. «Paid no heed to» means did not listen to or did not care about. So the Rat ignored his own sad blame. Check by swapping in your meaning: "The Rat ignored his sad blaming of himself" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: The phrase «beyond reproach» is different. It means so good that nobody can criticise it, as in «behaviour beyond reproach». Here reproach means criticism, so the phrase means there is nothing to find fault with.
fact: Reproach can be a verb (to reproach someone) or a noun (a reproach).
fact: A reproach shows disappointment or blame, often gently or sadly.
fact: «Beyond reproach» means so good that no one can criticise it.
fact: «Doleful» means sad, and «self-reproaches» means blaming yourself.
fact: «Reproach yourself» means to feel guilty about what you did.
exercise: «The teacher looked at the muddy boots with reproach.» What does reproach mean in this sentence? | Showing she was in a hurry | Showing she was cold | Showing great pride | *Showing disappointment or blame
exercise: «Her work was so careful and neat that it was beyond reproach.» What does beyond reproach mean? | Very late and very messy | *So good that no one can criticise it | Hidden under a rock | Too far away to see
exercise: «The Rat paid no heed to his doleful self-reproaches.» What does self-reproaches mean? | Praising yourself | Hiding from other people | *Blaming yourself | Sleeping late in the morning
test: «Mum did not shout, but her gentle reproach made Sam feel sorry.» What is a reproach here? | *Words or a look showing disappointment | A new bicycle | A loud cheer
test: Which word means to tell someone, gently or sadly, that you are disappointed in what they did? | Hide | Praise | *Reproach | Forget
exam: Which sentence uses reproach as a verb? | Her reproaches were beyond belief, and nobody listened. | The look of reproach on his face said enough. | The reproach hung in the air. | *I will reproach him, gently, for breaking the window.
